rd68021_seq: a level-7 interrupt taken from STOP is taken once, not twice

irq_prev_q, the level the level-7 edge is judged against, was updated only
when a decode retired.  Leaving STOP for an interrupt retires no decode, so
it kept the level from before the STOP.  A level-7 request is held by the
device until the handler clears it, so at the handler's first instruction
boundary the level was still 7 against a remembered lower level: a fresh
"edge", and the non-maskable interrupt was taken again, nested.

Seen on the Sun-3/60 replica with SunOS 4.1.1: the kernel idles in STOP
while the PROM monitor's 100 Hz level-7 clock still runs; one tick gave two
level-7 acknowledges 6 us apart, the inner monitor handler read and cleared
the clock chip's status, and the outer one then found it clear and reported
"Exception 0x7C at 0E09C19A" (idle).

Now the level is also recorded on the step from STOP into the interrupt.

Against RD68021 33d9289.  To drop once upstream carries it.

--- a/rtl/rd68021_seq.sv
+++ b/rtl/rd68021_seq.sv
@@ -2601,10 +2601,15 @@
     end
   end
 
-  // The level as it was at the last boundary, for the level-7 edge.
+  // The level as it was at the last boundary, for the level-7 edge -- and at
+  // the step out of STOP into the interrupt, which retires no decode: without
+  // it a level-7 request taken from STOP is still an "edge" at the handler's
+  // first boundary (the device holds IPL until the handler clears it), and
+  // the non-maskable interrupt is taken a second time, nested.
   always_ff @(posedge clk or negedge rst_n) begin
     if (!rst_n)                                        irq_prev_q <= 3'd0;
-    else if (retire && (`UF(SEQ) == rd68021_ucode_pkg::U_SEQ_DECODE))
+    else if ((retire && (`UF(SEQ) == rd68021_ucode_pkg::U_SEQ_DECODE))
+             || (stopped_q && irq_pending))
                                                        irq_prev_q <= irq_level;
   end
 
